        {
          "id": "D-005",
          "title": "The stage clock tracks the SERVER's staged item, not the local product selection",
          "body": "Date: 2026-08-17\nDISCOVERED while implementing P-001. SideStage currently has TWO different notions of \"on stage\" and they can disagree:\n\n1. `RunOfShowPanel` (Studio dock) gets its `StageLog` from `SellerTab.tsx:200`, which advances on `selectedProductId` — a purely LOCAL selection owned by `App.tsx:54` and set via `onActiveProductChange`. Its \"Take live\" button is `onStageNext={(productId) => onActiveProductChange(productId)}` (RunOfShowPanel.tsx:427): it changes the local selection and NOTHING ELSE. It does not call `executeSellerAction`, so it never stages anything server-side despite its label.\n\n2. `EventLineupGrid` / the Lineup section uses `item.onStage` — server-authoritative state that Push and Swap actually mutate through `executeSellerAction`.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THIS PLAN: the Lineup timeline shows commerce state and its Push button mutates the server, so its \"ON STAGE\" chip must reflect server truth. If the shared clock kept advancing on the local selection, the timeline could render \"ON STAGE\" on one product while the clock timed a different one — the UI contradicting itself, and an authoritative-looking pace number computed from the wrong slot. That is exactly the failure D-003 exists to prevent, one level deeper than D-003 anticipated.\n\nDECISION: the shared stage clock advances on the EVENT'S STAGED PRODUCT (server truth, `items.find(i => i.onStage)`), not on `selectedProductId`. \"Run of show\" means the show, not a selection. `SellerTab` already sits above both consumers and `RunOfShowPanel` already runs the `event.actions.items` query, so sourcing it at the provider is hoisting an existing read, not adding one.\n\nCONSEQUENCE ACCEPTED AND FIXED: under the new clock the dock panel's \"Take live\" would visibly stop moving the clock — my change would expose the pre-existing gap as an apparent regression. So P-009 makes \"Take live\" do what its label says: push the item server-side via the same guarded `executeSellerAction` path the Lineup uses, AND keep setting the local selection so transcript focus still follows. Fixing the button is part of this change, not a follow-up: shipping the clock without it would leave a button that lies.\n\nThe advisory contract from D-002 still holds — nothing here gates or auto-drives staging; off-plan and revisited products stay representable.\nRelated: P-001, P-004",
          "date": "2026-08-17",
          "itemRefs": [
            "P-001",
            "P-009",
            "P-004"
          ],
          "lineNumber": 112
        },
        {
          "id": "D-006",
          "title": "D-006 — Every markdown price comes from markdown-guard.ts, and it CEILS; the preview price IS the sent price",
          "body": "Date: 2026-08-17\nAny surface that offers a markdown (the Lineup timeline, the Studio dock panel, anything later) computes its price through `apps/web/src/seller/markdown-guard.ts` and sends the exact cents the preview displayed. No surface recomputes a price of its own.\n\nWHY — two defects found while building P-002, both verified by reading the writers rather than inferred:\n\n1. ROUND vs CEIL. `EventManager.tsx` sent `Math.max(1, Math.round(price * (1 - pct/100)))`, while the server derives its per-product floor with `Math.ceil` (`event-config.service.ts:176`) and then rejects anything strictly below it (`guardrail.ts:129`). For any price where `price * (1 - pct/100)` has a fractional part below .5, a markdown at EXACTLY the event limit lands one cent under the floor and is refused. Concrete: price 2003, cap 20% → floor ceil(1602.4)=1603, sent round(1602.4)=1602 → blocked 'price-floor'. The seller dials in the limit the UI advertised and the server says no. Ceiling satisfies the floor and the cap simultaneously at the boundary, so the maximum markdown becomes reachable.\n\n2. THE FLOOR IS NOT ON THE WIRE. `readEventConfigView` sends the policy WITHOUT running the floor derivation — `policyFromConfig` returns `priceFloorCentsByProduct: {}` and the derivation happens later, at the action boundary (`event-policy-resolver.ts:45`). So a client that wants to draw the floor must derive it the same way: explicit policy floor wins, else `max(1, ceil(price * (1 - cap/100)))`. The prior P-002 note in WI-39609 claimed `EventConfigView.policy` already carried `priceFloorCentsByProduct` — it did not; it declared only `automationLevel` and `maxMarkdownPercent`. That claim is RETRACTED. The field has now been added to the type (it IS on the wire, just usually empty) with a comment explaining why it is empty.\n\nHOW IT IS HELD: `markdown-guard.test.ts` is DIFFERENTIAL, not a restatement — it imports the real `PolicyActionGuard` and the real `withDerivedPriceFloors` and sweeps prices × caps × floors × percents against them. It earned its place immediately: its first run failed and exposed two genuine errors in my own mirror (I compared the REQUESTED percent to the cap, where the server compares the ACHIEVED percent after ceiling to whole cents; and I checked the cap before the floor, where the server checks the floor first). A hand-written restatement of the rule would have agreed with both mistakes.\n\nONE DELIBERATE DIVERGENCE, pinned by its own test: the control is STRICTER than the server for a request above the stated cap that ceiling would sneak back under it (33.5% off $1.99 arrives as 33.17%, which a 33.3% cap accepts). The UI stops at the stated cap — letting it exceed a stated policy limit on a rounding artefact would be dishonest. The server-facing verdict is unaffected.\n\nDEGRADE HONESTLY: with no policy loaded the control draws NO floor, NO limit and NO track, says \"guardrails have not loaded — the server still enforces\", and still lets the seller send. Inventing a limit we never read, or blocking on a client-side unknown, are both worse than saying so.\nRelated: P-002, P-006",
          "date": "2026-08-17",
          "itemRefs": [
            "P-002",
            "P-006"
          ],
          "lineNumber": 129
        },
        {
          "id": "D-007",
          "title": "D-007 — The buyer picker is sourced from server-seen presence, and the offer's PRICE is guarded on the same path",
          "body": "Date: 2026-08-17\nThree rulings from building P-003. All bind P-006 (the Studio dock adopts this component, no forked copy).\n\n**1. Candidates come from `event.chat.presence` + the live auction bidder — and an empty room DISABLES send rather than falling back to free text.**\n`buyerCandidates()` (apps/web/src/seller/offer-guard.ts) folds room presence (server-side TTL 35s, chat.service.ts:54) with the live auction's `winnerOrder.bidderId`, deduped, room-first, most-recently-seen first. The old control was a text input whose ONLY server check is non-empty (guardrail.ts:78), so a typo composed a perfectly valid offer addressed to nobody and failed silently downstream. When nobody is in the room the picker says so and Send stays disabled — an offer to an absent buyer is not a capability worth preserving. `loading` is distinguished from `empty` because \"not known yet\" and \"nobody is here\" say opposite things to a seller and only one is a reason to stop.\n\n**2. A targeted offer is a PRICE-BEARING kind, so the price floor + markdown cap apply to it — the offer price was previously unguarded.**\n`PRICE_KINDS` (guardrail.ts:16) includes `targeted-offer`, so an offer runs the identical floor/cap gate a markdown does (guardrail.ts:121-145). The offer price field was a free number with no guardrail: a seller could type a price the event policy had already ruled out and learn about it only from a server rejection. `evaluateOffer` therefore REUSES `markdown-guard.ts`'s `effectiveFloorCents` + `achievedMarkdownPercent` rather than forking them, and mirrors the server's full order (blocked-kind → buyer → quantity → availability → price → floor → cap → not-above-event-price). No policy reached the client ⇒ stays sendable and says the server still enforces; inventing a guardrail we never read is the failure mode this avoids.\n\n**3. FIXED a false block: the offer quantity was bounded by the RESERVED count, the server checks AVAILABILITY.**\n`positiveWholeNumber(draft.offerQuantity, Math.max(1, row.quantity))` capped the offer at reserved units, but guardrail.ts:84 compares against `eventItem.availableQty`. Wherever availability exceeded the reserved count the control refused an offer the server would have allowed. Now bounded by `availableQty` (both the parse and the input's `max`), so the client is neither stricter nor looser than the server.\n\n**Verification.** `offer-guard.test.ts` is DIFFERENTIAL against the real `PolicyActionGuard` (16 composed offers, client `sendable` asserted equal to server `allowed`), and carries two anti-vacuity guards the markdown suite lacked: a both-verdicts assertion, and a PERMANENT calibration control — a deliberately-wrong mirror reproducing the OLD behaviour (non-empty buyer + parseable numbers) asserted to DIVERGE from the server on >2 cases. Without that control a matrix that drifted toward all-allow would keep passing while the mirror rotted. The repo's falsifiability guidance names this tier-1 pattern precisely because mutating the shared tree to prove a guard can fail is unsafe here (the git-sync sweep commits the mutant).\nRelated: P-003, P-006",
          "date": "2026-08-17",
          "itemRefs": [
            "P-003",
            "P-006"
          ],
          "lineNumber": 146
        },
        {
          "id": "D-008",
          "title": "D-008 — \"Take live\" pushes server-side and only then follows; deliberately NO client mirror of the push guard",
          "body": "Date: 2026-08-17\nP-010 implemented in apps/web/src/seller/RunOfShowPanel.tsx. `onStageNext` was `(productId) => onActiveProductChange(productId)` — local selection ONLY, with `executeSellerAction` not even imported. Under D-005's server-truth clock that button was visibly inert: the seller tapped it, the card changed, and the clock never started because nothing on the server had moved.\n\n**The ORDER is the correctness argument.** The guarded push (`kind:'push'`) is awaited FIRST; only on success does the panel invalidate `event.actions.items` and then advance local selection. Advancing first — or optimistically — would put the dock and the clock into exactly the disagreement D-005 exists to prevent, and a REFUSED push would leave the seller looking at a card that is not on stage. A test pins that failure mode directly: a rejecting `executeSellerAction` must leave `onActiveProductChange` uncalled and `itemsQuery.invalidate` uncalled, with the server's refusal shown verbatim.\n\n**NO client mirror of the push guard — and this is a deliberate limit on the D-006/D-007 pattern, not an oversight.** The markdown and offer mirrors exist because the seller COMPOSES a value the server may refuse (a percent, a price, a quantity, a buyer), so the control has to stop where the server stops or it lies about what is sendable. \"Take live\" composes nothing: it is one button on one already-planned product. There is no value to pre-validate, so the honest design is to CALL the server and report its verdict verbatim rather than predict it. Mirroring here would be a parallel guard over an empty input space — the reuse-first smell, not an application of it. What the client DOES guarantee is that the proposal is well-formed: a push carries no quantity, no price, and no swap target (guardrail.ts:101-118 blocks each), and always carries a reason (:70, checked before any kind-specific branch). The test asserts all four properties of the emitted action rather than trusting the call site.\n\n**Prop change.** `RunOfShowPanelProps` gains a required `actorId`; SellerTab passes `userId`, which the two other panel props there already use. Both `RunOfShowPanel.integration.test.tsx` mount sites updated. The dock and mobile hosts spread the same props object, so neither needed a change.\n\n**Verification.** apps/web tsc EXIT=0; RunOfShowPanel integration + unit + seller-panels + SellerTab.studio = 4 files / 25 tests green; FULL web workspace suite 90 files / 739 tests green. The two new tests genuinely falsify against the old code rather than merely passing: the previous implementation never imported `executeSellerAction`, so `toHaveBeenCalledOnce()` is unsatisfiable against it.\n\n**Measurement note worth keeping.** A full-suite run during this item reported ~60 files FAILING at COLLECTION (`FAIL src/X [ src/X ]` — an import error shape, not assertions), which read exactly like a catastrophic shared-tree break. It was a RACE: a peer was mid-write in the same tree and vitest collected a half-saved module. The immediately-following identical run was 90/90 green. Do not report a suite-wide collection failure in this shared tree without a second run — and note the same race is the likeliest explanation of the transient `event-lifecycle.test.ts` red seen earlier, which is also now green.\nRelated: P-010, P-001, P-006",
          "date": "2026-08-17",
          "itemRefs": [
            "P-010",
            "P-001",
            "P-006"
          ],
          "lineNumber": 162
        },
        {
          "id": "D-009",
          "title": "P-004 is gated on P-001's CODE, not on P-001's tests — otherwise the dependency graph deadlocks",
          "body": "Date: 2026-08-17\nDate: 2026-08-17\n[self-imposed — a sequencing ruling, not an owner directive]\n\nP-004 read `blocked` with its only unresolved blocker being P-001, and P-001 is deliberately `wip`. Taken literally that is a DEADLOCK, by construction of this plan's own edges: P-001's checkpoint says it \"closes under P-007 once its tests exist\", and P-007 is blocked by P-005, which is blocked by P-004, which is blocked by P-001. Nothing in that cycle can ever start.\n\nThe cycle is an artifact of one item carrying two different obligations. Separating them resolves it:\n\n- P-001's CODE — the thing D-003 actually requires of it (\"P-001 lifts the StageLog to ONE shared provider that both the Studio dock panel and the Lineup timeline read\") — is LANDED and verified. `apps/web/src/seller/stage-clock.tsx` exports StageClockProvider / useStageClock / useHasStageClock; `SellerTab.tsx` drives it off the server-authoritative staged product per D-005; apps/web `tsc --noEmit` exits 0. The last caveat P-001 carried (the dock's \"Take live\" was inert, so a server-truth clock would have nothing to advance on) was closed by P-010, now `done` and green.\n- P-001's TESTS are owned by P-007 by explicit prior decision, and P-007's scope is the tests for ALL the new components — P-004's included. So P-001's test cannot precede P-004's existence.\n\nRULING: the D-003 blocking relationship is satisfied by a WORKING shared clock, which exists. P-004 proceeds now. P-001 stays `wip` — it is NOT being marked done, because a clean typecheck is not a test and closing it now would be an under-evidenced completion. P-001 and P-004 both close under P-007, together, when their tests land.\n\nWHAT THIS DOES NOT LICENSE: the D-003 fallback still binds. If the shared clock turns out not to work when the timeline actually consumes it, the response is to OMIT the pace display from the Lineup and say so — never to derive a second StageLog locally. `useHasStageClock()` exists precisely so the timeline can render \"no clock\" as a legible state instead of a wrong number, and P-004 renders pace only when it returns true.\n\nRelated: P-001, P-004, P-007\nRelated: P-001, P-004, P-007",
          "date": "2026-08-17",
          "itemRefs": [
            "P-004",
            "P-001",
            "P-007",
            "P-005",
            "P-010"
          ],
          "lineNumber": 177
        },
        {
          "id": "D-010",
          "title": "An event nobody planned still has a show — an unsaved plan seeds the show order from the LINEUP",
          "body": "Date: 2026-08-17\nDate: 2026-08-17\n[self-imposed — a product ruling discovered while wiring P-005]\n\nDISCOVERED by reading the rendered markup, not by reasoning about it. With the timeline mounted and no stored run-of-show, EventManager rendered \"Nothing is in the show yet\", every reserved product in the \"Reserved · not in the show\" tray, and — because the commerce controls live in a per-SLOT drawer — NO push, markdown, stock, auction or offer control anywhere on the screen. A seller who never opened the old planner could not run their show at all.\n\nThat is direction C's premise inverted. If the lineup IS the run of show (D-001), then a lineup with items IS a show with slots; seeding empty re-creates the two-surface split the plan exists to remove, with the authoring step now MANDATORY instead of optional.\n\nTHE RULE\n\n- A plan that was NEVER SAVED seeds the show order from the LINEUP: every reserved product becomes a slot, in lineup order, with no minutes budget and no notes. The tray is consequently empty, and the timeline is immediately usable.\n- A plan that WAS saved seeds VERBATIM — including a deliberately emptied one. Removing a product from the show has to survive a reload, or \"remove\" silently means \"remove until refresh\".\n- Nothing is written on seed. This is local draft state; the document changes only when the seller saves.\n\nWHY THE TWO CASES ARE DISTINGUISHABLE (read the writer, don't infer)\n\n`event.runOfShow` never returns nothing: `RunOfShowService.get` falls back to `emptyRunOfShow(id)`, which stamps `updatedAt: new Date(0).toISOString()` (run-of-show.service.ts:76), while every real save stamps `new Date().toISOString()` (:105). `emptyRunOfShow` has exactly one caller — that fallback — so an epoch `updatedAt` with zero entries means \"no row exists\", and is not reachable from a genuine save. The seed therefore branches on `entries.length === 0 && Date.parse(updatedAt) === 0`, not on emptiness alone, which would have resurrected products the seller deliberately removed.\n\nOne ordering trap this closes: the lineup query and the plan query resolve independently, so seeding while `items` is still empty would pin an empty show and never re-seed (the seed is once-per-event). The never-saved branch therefore WAITS for a non-empty lineup.\n\nBINDS P-006 AND P-007\n\nP-006 (the dock adopts this component) must seed the same way — a dock that starts empty while the Lineup starts populated is two answers to \"what is my show\". P-007 owns the regressions: an unplanned event renders slots and an empty tray; a saved-then-emptied plan renders an empty show and a full tray; and the epoch sentinel itself, so a server that ever stops stamping the epoch fails a test here rather than silently resurrecting removed products.\nRelated: P-005, P-006, P-007",
          "date": "2026-08-17",
          "itemRefs": [
            "P-005",
            "P-006",
            "P-007"
          ],
          "lineNumber": 196
        },
        {
          "id": "D-011",
          "title": "D-011 — Targeted offers compose through ONE shared OfferComposer; no surface assembles the controls itself",
          "body": "Date: 2026-08-17\nP-006's \"no forked copies\" turned out to bind more than the two named controls, and the drift it warns about had ALREADY happened by the time this item was picked up.\n\nWHAT WAS FOUND. The Lineup timeline (P-004) assembled its own offer block: BuyerPicker + qty + price + a Send button guarded by `draft.offerBuyerId !== '' && priceToCents(...) !== null`. The `EventLineupGrid` it replaced had guarded the SAME action with `evaluateOffer(...)`. So the migration silently DROPPED the price guard: the timeline offered to send below-floor offers the server refuses, and offers above the verified event price. Nothing failed — the only offer coverage was the blocked-kind case — which is why an inline copy is the wrong shape here even when it looks like four ordinary inputs.\n\nTHE RULING. `seller/OfferComposer.tsx` is the ONLY place a targeted offer may be composed. It owns the buyer picker, the qty/price fields, the `evaluateOffer` mirror, the disabled state AND the stated reason. A surface passes data in and receives `(buyer, quantity, priceCents)` out; it does not decide sendability. Both surfaces now go through it: the Lineup drawer and the Studio dock.\n\nWHY A GUARD BELONGS IN THE COMPONENT, NOT THE CALLER. An offer is four independently-refusable values (buyer the server has seen, quantity within availability, price at/above floor and not above event price, action kind permitted). A caller that assembles the controls inevitably guards SOME of them; the ones it forgets become a button enabled for an action the server rejects. Centralising it means a surface CANNOT forget, rather than being trusted to remember.\n\nBINDS: any future surface that sends offers (mobile studio, copilot suggestions). It also binds P-007 — the composer's guard has its own falsifiable tests (`OfferComposer.test.tsx`), each blocked case paired with a sendable one on the same policy so a permanently-dead button cannot pass.\n\nThe mirror never replaces the server. `evaluateOffer` exists so the control stops where the server stops; the server remains the authority that refuses.\nRelated: P-006, P-004, P-007",
          "date": "2026-08-17",
          "itemRefs": [
            "P-006",
            "P-004",
            "P-007"
          ],
          "lineNumber": 222
        },
        {
          "id": "D-012",
          "title": "D-012 — D-003's \"one clock\" means one LOG and one PULSE; the render tick lives in the provider",
          "body": "Date: 2026-08-17\nD-003 was implemented as a shared `StageLog` in `StageClockProvider`, and that was read as satisfied. It was not. Two things were still split, and each is a way for the surfaces to disagree.\n\n(1) A SECOND ACCUMULATOR. `SellerTab` kept its own `runOfShowLog` state, advanced by its own `stageLogOnProductChange` effect, and passed it to the dock as a `stageLog` PROP — while the Lineup read the provider. Two `StageLog`s, both live. A StageLog is ACCUMULATED state, so two of them agree only by luck of having mounted together.\n\n(2) TWO PULSES. `EventManager` and `RunOfShowPanel` each ran their OWN `setInterval(..., 1000)` to re-render pace. Independent phases: the same elapsed second could paint on one surface up to a second before the other. Two clocks in effect, even with the log shared.\n\nTHE RULING. The provider owns BOTH: the log and the `nowMs` pulse. Surfaces read `useStageClock()` (log) and `useStageNow()` (instant) and create NEITHER. The dock's `stageLog` prop is GONE — deleted, not deprecated — so a host cannot hand a panel a private log. The pulse stays gated on `log.activeProductId`, so an idle show costs no timer.\n\nENFORCEMENT, so this cannot silently regress: `sync-contract.test.ts`'s `legacyAccessBudget` now carries exactly ONE stage-clock timer entry, on `seller/stage-clock.tsx`. The entries on `seller/RunOfShowPanel.tsx` and `events/EventManager.tsx` were REMOVED rather than left as harmless ceilings — an unused ceiling passes vacuously (0 <= 1), so leaving them would let a surface re-grow its own timer with the guard still green. A new timer on either file is now an UNKNOWN finding and fails the suite. Measured after the change: 1 timer in stage-clock.tsx, 0 in both surfaces.\n\nBINDS P-007 (the clock's tests close with P-001) and any future surface that renders pace: read the provider, never start a ticker.\nRelated: P-001, P-006, P-007",
          "date": "2026-08-17",
          "itemRefs": [
            "P-007",
            "P-001",
            "P-006"
          ],
          "lineNumber": 237
        }
